  "documentation": {
    "range": "Within the European region, this species is widespread across the continent and absent only from Ireland and Denmark, although it is a regular migrant in the latter. In Britain it only occurs in the Norfolk Broads in eastern England. It is found at altitudes between sea level and 1,500 m.<br/><br/>In the EU 27 region, this species' area of occupancy is 152,612 km² and the extent of occurrence is 8,625,911 km².<br/><br/>The global distribution of this species extends across the Palearctic and Nearctic. The global distribution area of the species is situated both within and outside Europe.",
    "population": "This is a common and widespread species in its European range. Declines in distribution or population have been reported in several countries. It is classed as Regionally Extinct in Denmark (although now a regular migrant); and Near Threatened in Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia, Romania and the United Kingdom (Maes <em>et al. </em>2019).",
    "habitats": "The Swallowtail is a butterfly of flower-rich meadows and small-scale farmland. The males and females meet at hilltops or above high trees, a type of behaviour known as 'hill-topping', with the butterflies dancing up and down in the air. The Swallowtail needs a lot of nectar. In the south of the range, they also occur in parks and gardens. Eggs are laid on various umbellifers, including Wild Carrot (<em>Daucus carota</em>) and <em>Rutaceae</em>. The British population in the Norfolk Broads only uses Milk Parsley (<em>Peucedanum palustre</em>) as a larval hostplant. The fully-grown larvae are very conspicuous, bright green with black stripes and orange spots. When disturbed they can suddenly show a Y-shaped, orange scent-gland or osmeterium, just behind its head, producing a strong, unpleasant smell. The caterpillars from autumn generations leave the hostplant to pupate on the ground and hibernation takes place as a pupa. The caterpillars of the summer generations pupate low down on the larval hostplant. Depending on the altitude of the breeding ground and its position in the distribution range, the Swallowtail produces one to three generations a year. Habitats are mesophile grasslands (9%), dry calcareous grasslands and steppes (8%), humid grasslands and tall herb communities (8%), fallow land, waste places (8%), urban parks and large gardens (7%), orchards, groves and tree plantations (6%) (van Swaay <em>et al. </em>2006).",
    "threats": "This species is not believed to face major threats at the European level, but it is declining in several countries though the reasons need further research. In the United Kingdom the Swallowtail only breeds in wet fenland habitat, where it is threatened by reduced water tables leading to scrub invasion and by ground and surface water pollution from nitrate and phosphate fertilisers. In the longer-term climate change and rising sea levels could lead to seawater incursion of the freshwater habitat.",
    "measures": "This species occurs in a number of protected areas across its range; and only in protected areas in the United Kingdom. No specific conservation actions are needed at the European level, but in countries where the species is declining, important habitats should be protected and managed, especially to prevent the loss or degradation of wet fenland breeding sites. The effects of conservation actions should be monitored by butterfly monitoring schemes.",
    "use_trade": "All butterflies are collected to some extent, but only for the extremely rare species can it be a problem and the trade in Europe is generally at a low level compared to other continents. There is no specific trade information for this species.",
    "rationale": "European regional assessment: Least Concern (LC)<br/>EU 27 regional assessment: Least Concern (LC)<br/><br/>Within the European region, the Swallowtail is widespread across the continent. This species is listed as Least Concern, as it has been evaluated against all the IUCN criteria and does not meet the thresholds for any category of threat in Europe or the EU 27 region.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "The taxon <em>saharae</em> was described as a subspecies of <em>Papilio machaon</em> by Oberthür (1879) and was later promoted to species level by Pittaway <em>et al</em>. (1994). However, the placement of the taxon has remained debated, with some authors treating it as a valid species, others as a subspecies of <em>P. machaon</em>. The taxon was assessed for the European and Mediterranean Red Lists as a subspecies of <em>P. machaon</em> by van Swaay <em>et al</em>. (2010, 2014), and the subspecies status of <em>saharae</em> was confirmed by Dupuis and Sperling (2020), though its species status is still followed by some authors (e.g., Cassar <em>et al</em>. 2023)."
  },
